A||||5||speed1||engine conversions for californian emissions?||||5||
Z||000000||speed1||05-14-2001||03:55 PM||||I've got an '87 GT 5spd and the old 2.8 is pretty much worn out and leaking oil like a sieve.  I am looking for an engine swap that would not be too expensive.  I was looking at doing the 3800SC, but I read terryk's post about smogging problems with manual trannies and swapped engines.  Anybody in California with an v8 or 3800SC and manual tranny want to share their experiences?||||reg||5
Z||000001||speed1||05-14-2001||11:57 PM||||There must be someone in California that has done and engine swap with a manual tranny.  Terryk, tgowens, cmwest, archie, any recommendations?||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||05-15-2001||01:30 AM||||Did you need to know more? Unless the ref is brain-dead, they won't certify a manual trans on an engine that never came with a manual trans in California. <P>They will take the VIN you give them for the donor engine and enter it into a system. It better come back with the engine, trans combo and California emissions option (YF5). A Fed engine won't pass. <P>Next they will look the engine up in the Mitchell's manual and check off all of the emissions components. They have to all be there.  <P>Next they will connect the scanner and make sure there are no emissions error codes and that it's closed loop. This is another reason for needing the VIN.<P>One little detail. Unless the ref is jerk, they will usually take the VIN you give them and not compare it to the VIN stamped on the engine to make sure they are the same. As it was said to me....."Don't make me lie. You lie.". Just have a California VIN to give them that matches the engine and trans. <A HREF="http://www.copartfinder.com" TARGET=_blank>www.copartfinder.com</A>  is a beautiful place to find VIN's...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Call BAR and get the straight scoop. The phone number is on the web. Search for Smog Check II in California. I talked with them at length several times first. A manual trans on a 3800SC won't fly. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||speed1||05-15-2001||03:11 AM||||terrry thanks for the info on the 3800SC. I guess I can scratch that swap off my list.  Too bad, I really liked the 3800SC.  It looks like my only option is the small block v8 swap.  It would give the most bang for the buck.  California smog rules really put a damper on engine swaps.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||05-15-2001||11:50 AM||||Actually, California rules don't put a damper on swaps. BAR gets a kick out of the swaps. Just use the right transmission.<P>Yes, if you are bent on a manual, that limits the options since not all engines were certified in CA with manuals.<P>Another little detail is that they don't seem to care *what* transmission is used. If you took an CA manual trans LT1 that came with a 6 speed and bolted it to a Getrag, they don't seem to care. <P>It's too bad GM didn't use a manual on the Buick V6. I heard rumors that the Calais had a manual on the MPFI 3.0L, but I could never find one.<P>If you've seen our air, the rules don't bother me at all. <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||05-15-2001||01:09 PM||||you could do a 3.4l swap, not asmuch of a power boost, but cheaper, easier, and the emission guys won't be able to knowtice the difference.||||reg||1
